Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report  MS 09  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  CASE  MS 09  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  Date Taken  REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit  Breast CTR Surg WAC 240 MEEI MRN  REMOVED_DATE Path Subspecialty Service  Breast 1 Results To  CONNIE ROCHE  NP KENNY C LAI  M D  RACHEL ANN HITT  M D  Resident  Matija Snuderl  M D  Signed Out by  Ronald Balassanian  M D  CLINICAL DATA  The patient is a 56 year old woman with very dense breast with indeterminate focus of enhancement seen in the medial left breast on MRI  FINAL DIAGNOSIS  A  BREAST  LEFT   CORE BIOPSY 1  INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  WELL DIFFERENTIATED GRADE REMOVED_DATE 2  DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  DCIS   MICROPAPILLARY TYPE  GRADE REMOVED_DATE 3  FIBROCYSTIC CHANGE WITH APOCRINE METAPLASIA  SEE NOTE  Note  Sections of the core biopsy reveal a rare cluster of angulated ducts infiltrating the stroma which are present over an area measuring approximately 0 2cm  Adjacent to the lesion is a 0 3cm cluster of ducts which are expanded by a proliferation of monomorphic ductal cells arranged in a micropapillary pattern lining the duct structures  The morphologic findings are compatible with a diagnosis of invasive tubular carcinoma arising in association with low grade micropapillary ductal carcinoma in situ  To help confirm these diagnoses  we performed level sections and a panel of immunostains on the core biopsy  Multiple level sections show the DCIS to better advantage  An immunostain for keratin REMOVED_DATE shows focal loss of staining in luminal cells and supports the diagnosis of DCIS  Immunohistochemical stains for cytokeratin  p63  myosin and calponin performed on slide  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  demonstrate absence of the myoepithelial layer in the infiltrating ducts and supports the diagnosis of invasive ductal carcinoma  Overall  the findings are limited due to limited sampling of the lesion  however  based on morphology and immunohistochemistry  the findings are compatible with well differentiated invasive ductal carcinoma and low grade ductal carcinoma in situ  Dr  David Wilbur has reviewed this case and concurs with the diagnosis  GROSS DESCRIPTION  The specimen is received in formalin   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME   REMOVE_MRN only is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 1cm  aggregate of multiple tan yellow to gray white fibroadipose tissue cores  which are submitted in toto  in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Final Diagnosis by Ronald Balassanian M D   Electronically signed on Monday August 31   REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 12 51 13PM By his her signature above  the pathologist listed as making the Final Diagnosis certifies that he she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed or corrected the diagnoses  PART   Breast  Left    Core biopsy  report_end  , Absent.
