Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Corrected Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  17 REMOVED_MONTH 94 ACCESSIONED ON  17 REMOVED_MONTH 94 at 11 16 CLINICAL DATA  Microcalcifications in breast on mammography      REVISED REPORT     Reason for Revision  CORRECTED CONSULTING PATHOLOGIST Revised by  John H  Eichhorn  M D  Revised on 22 REMOVED_MONTH 94 at 08 53                                                                                     FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  LEFT   BIOPSY  1  INFILTRATING CARCINOMA  0 9 CM   TUBULOLOBULAR TYPE  WITH A MINOR COMPONENT IN SITU CARCINOMA  SEE NOTE  2  FIBROCYSTIC CHANGES  INCLUDING DUCTAL EPITHELIAL HYPERPLASIA  SCLEROSING ADENOSIS  FIBROSIS AND CYSTS  3  ABUNDANT INTRALUMINAL MICROCALCIFICATIONS  Note  Because of an insidious pattern of infiltration  low grade cytological features and a lack of discrete demarcation on gross inspection  it is difficult to reliably establish the borders of this tumor  Measured from slide A  the greatest extent is 0 9cm  The examined inked margins appear to be free of tumor  Neither lymphatic nor vascular invasion is identified  The minor component of in situ carcinoma may be ductal or lobular in type  and is in close proximity to the invasion component  Immunostains for estrogen and progesterone receptors will be performed and the results reported as an addendum  This subtype of carcinoma has behaved somewhat worse than classical tubular carcinoma  but better than the usual infiltrating lobular carcinoma  according to the literature  Reference  Fisher ER  et al  Tubulolobular invasive breast cancer  a variant of lobular invasive cancer  Human Pathology 8 679 683  Diagnosis by  John H  Eichhorn  M D  Signed On  22 REMOVED_MONTH 94 at 13 07 Consulting Pathologist s   Frederick Carl Koerner  M D                                                                                      RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast  Left   The tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein  The tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein  See note  Note  In this laboratory  we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way  paraffin sections  and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay  91  of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  96  of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections  the predictive value of a positive result was 97  and the predictive value of a negative result was 95   In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  THE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN ARE FOR INVESTIGATIONAL USE ONLY  THEY MUST NOT BE USED FOR CLINICAL PURPOSES  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  02 REMOVED_MONTH 94 at 09 25    REVISED REMOVED_DATE    GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name and unit number only  is a 7 x 2 x 3cm yellow tan piece of soft tissue  An accompanying specimen x ray shows microcalcifications  The specimen is inked and bisected to reveal normal fibrofatty breast parenchyma  and is submitted entirely in A through F  Resident  Ajit J  Alles  MD  PhD     INTRA OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS     GROSS DIAGNOSIS  LEFT BREAST MASS   NO SUSPICIOUS LESION  O R  Resident  RICHARD S  HAMILTON  HMS O R  Diagnosis by  Eugene Jerome Mark  M D                                                                                  SOURCE CARE UNIT  SURGICAL DAY CARE   ACC 3 REPORTS TO  Leslie Wayne Ottinger  M D  PART   BREAST  LEFT MASS   BIOPSY   report_end  , Present.
